  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $-1,$ $0,$ and $1$ with probabilities $0.15,$ $0.55,$ and $0.3,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=-1]=4,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=0]=9,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]=8.\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\text{E}[X]?$",
  "worked_solution": "We are given the conditional expectations of $X$ given different values of another random variable $Y,$ along with the probabilities of those values. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes three values:\n\n- $Y=-1$ with probability $0.15,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=-1]=4$\n\n- $Y=0$ with probability $0.55,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=0]=9$\n\n- $Y=1$ with probability $0.3,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]=8$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=-1]\\cdot P(Y=-1)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=0]\\cdot P(Y=0) \\\\  & \\,+\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]\\cdot P(Y=1) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.15+9\\cdot 0.55+8\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.6+4.95+2.4 \\\\  & =7.95\\end{aligned}\n$$",
